### Bridge operations program: guarded unlock, set monitoring, fleet cap, proposer drain (2026-10-07)

A controlled rollout closed out the bridge hardening program on the live fleet. The four-attester epoch-1 set registered on all five nodes; the hub `aitbc-bridge-set` textfile exporter now reports `possible_signers=4` to Prometheus (`9812a91d51` object-form `VALIDATOR_SET` parse; `04b56ef9bb` 0644 output + latest-epoch-never-decays freshness matching `bridge_validator.py`), with `BridgeSetCannotMeetThreshold`/`BridgeSetStale` loaded and inactive.

- `BRIDGE_LOCKS_PAUSED=false` went live on all five hosts after the preconditions held (`possible_signers=4` + the registered epoch-1 set); `BRIDGE_MAX_LOCK_AMOUNT=36000000000` (1,000 AIT per-lock cap) rides with it in every `blockchain.env` and running rpc environment. Restart order: node2, node0, node1, hub1, hub.
- The retired proposer/proposer-account residue was drained in one `TRANSFER` (`0x54b547da…f2c` — 35,999,913,326,931,922 units to live `escrow_fee_recipient` `0x34FFfAd1…`, sealed at block 38368, nonce 90), keeping the 36,000,000,000-unit reserve. The account's ~36T-unit float no longer sits on a genesis-era identity.
- **Live result**: all five hosts at `04b56ef9bb`, `bridge/health` reporting `bridge_max_lock_amount: 36000000000`, `bridge_validators` latest epoch 1 with 4 active rows fleet-wide, no firing alerts, `BRIDGE_LOCKS_PAUSED=false` everywhere. The B43 persistence audit confirmed no deploy/update script can clobber these values; this version adds them to `blockchain.env.open-island` and documents the ops surface.

### Coordinator: dead SETTLEMENT_PRIVATE_KEY signer and startup gate removed

`SETTLEMENT_PRIVATE_KEY` was a v0.25.8 leftover: `SettlementHook._sign_settlement_message` signed a `SettlementMessage` (`{"job_id","amount","target_address"}`, canonical-JSON + keccak256 + secp256k1) for the never-wired cross-chain bridge path — `SettlementHook` is never instantiated, the `/settlement/cross-chain*` endpoints return 501, `BridgeManager._create_adapter` refuses every adapter, and `_verify_signature` is a stub returning `True`, so nothing on or off chain ever consumed the signature. The on-chain settlement authority is the node's `ESCROW_RELEASE_PRIVATE_KEY` (`escrow_settlement_authority`, `0x11741E1A…` since KR-2); the coordinator only POSTs `/rpc/escrow/{job_id}/release` and the node signs. The variable's sole live effect was the production startup gate in `validate_critical_environment_variables` — kept plausible by a stale `config.py` comment claiming the deployed hub runs `ENVIRONMENT=development` (it runs `production` via `aitbc-coordinator-api-override.env`; verified against the live process environment). The gate, the dead signer, the `3_coordinator-api.md` env line, and the stale test docstrings are removed together. `SETTLEMENT_PRIVATE_KEY` may be dropped from coordinator env files once hosts run this commit — it signs nothing and holds no funds, so the ordering matters only for the startup gate.

### CLI: `aitbc exchange prepay deposit` — node-tagged Sepolia ETH deposits for the prepaid-BUY lane (PREPAY-1)

D2's end-to-end match needs a BUY backed by a prepaid ETH balance, and no
safe CLI path existed to send the `EXCHANGE_DEPOSIT:<node address>` tagged
Sepolia ETH deposit the exchange monitor credits — `wallet bridge deposit`
writes the AIT bridge tag instead. The new command closes that gap without
key workarounds:

- `aitbc exchange prepay deposit --amount-eth X --wallet NAME` prints the
  unsigned transaction (chain id, recipient, value, the tag text, gas
  estimate, effective cap) and never touches a password or key — that is
  the default. `--send` signs once with the wallet's own decrypted key and
  broadcasts exactly once; a failed broadcast is reported, not retried.
- The recipient is always the `deposit_address` advertised by the exchange's
  own `/v1/bridge/status` — never typed by the operator.
- The input-data tag is `EXCHANGE_DEPOSIT:<node address>` as UTF-8, exactly
  what `wallet_app.bridge.bridge_monitor._parse_exchange_deposit_tag`
  parses; the test suite round-trips the produced hex through the real
  parser.
- Refusals: non-Sepolia chain id (must be 11155111), wallet not a
  `registered` node, missing or mismatched registry `eth_address`,
  `--amount-eth` above the per-call cap (default 0.01 ETH, raised only by
  an explicit `--max-eth`), amount+gas over the wallet's Sepolia balance,
  and — amended on review — a missing or unparseable `eth_gasPrice`:
  `--send` refuses rather than signing a zero-price transaction the
  network would reject, and the balance check can no longer pass on an
  assumed-zero gas cost. Over-cap refusals name the flag.
- The generated MCP tool `aitbc_exchange_prepay_deposit` lands in the
  `deposit` destructive class: `dry_run`/`confirm`-gated with
  `destructive_hint`, same as the other send paths.

Tests: 15 items in `cli/tests/test_exchange_prepay_deposit.py` — tag
round-trip through the real monitor parser, `--print` reading no key
material, wrong-chain/unregistered/eth_address-mismatch/empty-board
refusals, cap refuse/pass with `--max-eth`, `--send` signing and
broadcasting exactly once with the tag inside the signed blob, and
insufficient-balance refusal. `tests/cli/test_exchange_command_names.py`
adds the exchange-group name-collision guard (AST scan plus runtime
resolution of `prepay deposit`) mirroring the market-group test.

### Website: one canonical main menu on every page (WEB-1)

Each `website/*.html` used to hand-write its own `header-nav` block and
omit the link to itself, so the menu's items and order differed page to
page (Exchange missing on explorer sub-pages, Bridges only on exchange,
dashboards carrying a different set entirely).

- Canonical menu, identical markup on every page that has one:
  Home, Exchange, Explorer, Marketplace, Bridges, Alerts. The page's own
  item stays in place and is marked `class="is-current"
  aria-current="page"` — membership and order never change between pages.
- `customer-dashboard.html` / `shop-dashboard.html` keep the same
  6-item main menu and gain a secondary `dashboard-nav` row
  (Customer Dashboard ↔ Shop Dashboard, own page marked current).
- `follower-api-key-announcement.html` intentionally stays menu-less.
- `website/style.css`: `is-current` is a color-only state (border/text
  `var(--primary)`) — no padding, weight, or width change, so items never
  reflow. `flex-wrap: wrap` keeps the row tidy at phone width.
- `scripts/ops/sync-website-menu.py` stamps the menu between
  `<!-- aitbc-menu:start/end -->` markers (dashboards have their own
  `aitbc-dashboard-menu` pair), is idempotent, and has a `--check` mode
  that fails on drift. To change the menu, edit the `MENU` list in the
  script and re-run it.
- Guard test `tests/unit/test_website_menu_consistency.py` parses every
  page: same items in the same order, exactly one `aria-current="page"`
  pointing at the page's own path (dashboards: in the secondary row;
  pages outside the menu carry none), the allowlist is the only
  menu-less page, and `--check` must be clean.

### Website: page hygiene — landmarks, head metadata, deferred scripts (WEB-2)

- Every page now has a real landmark structure: the main menu is
  `<nav class="header-nav" aria-label="Main">` (inner markup unchanged
  from WEB-1), the dashboards' secondary row is
  `<nav class="dashboard-nav" aria-label="Dashboards">`, and each page's
  primary content sits in `<main id="main" tabindex="-1">`.
- A "Skip to content" link is the first focusable element in <body> on
  every page — including the menu-less announcement page — hidden with
  the clip technique (never display:none) and shown top-left with nav
  tokens + outline on focus. Stamped by the same generator between
  `aitbc-skip` markers.
- Head hygiene: `bridges.html` gains a correct viewport meta (its
  existing tag was a malformed `name="viewport=..."` attribute that
  never applied); titles normalize to "<Page name> — AITBC" everywhere
  except index (kept descriptive per operator); meta descriptions added
  to block, tx, search, and the follower announcement; `<link
  rel="canonical">` on the ten sitemap pages; `noindex` on the dynamic
  block/tx/search pages.
- Every `<script src>` tag is now `defer`red — all site scripts only
  touch the DOM from DOMContentLoaded handlers, so nothing blocks first
  paint and execution order is preserved. No page needed an exception.
- `tests/unit/test_website_menu_consistency.py` gains a hygiene suite:
  viewport/charset/lang, exactly one main and one main nav, skip link
  first, description and title pattern, canonical exactly on sitemap
  pages, noindex exactly on the dynamic pages.


### Website: dashboard init under deferred scripts (WEB-2b)

WEB-2's `defer` on `dashboard.js` broke both dashboard pages: their inline
scripts passed the bare `loadCustomerDashboard` / `loadShopDashboard`
identifier to `addEventListener`, evaluated at parse time before the
deferred file executes — a ReferenceError, so neither dashboard loaded.
WEB-2's own claim ("site scripts only touch the DOM from DOMContentLoaded
handlers") missed the inline listeners.

- `caa30f841` — both pages now wrap the init call in an arrow callback so
  the name resolves when the event fires (the smaller fix vs. moving init
  into `dashboard.js`; the inline script stays the sole init trigger, so
  still exactly one load). Audit of every page's inline `<script>`: only
  these two pages hit; block/tx/search inline blocks are self-contained
  with no external scripts, index's inline block is JSON-LD, the rest
  have no inline JS.
- `caa30f841` — new `tests/unit/test_website_deferred_scripts.py` fails
  any page whose inline script references a name declared top-level in a
  deferred script (or the `lucide`/`Chart` vendor globals) outside a
  callback or function body. Verified: fails with both violations on
  `7e59f6c13`, passes on the fix. Page-load emulation on node2 (`vm`
  contexts replaying parse→defer→DOMContentLoaded order): fixed pages
  init exactly once with no console errors; `7e59f6c13` pages throw
  ReferenceError and never init.


### Website: status strip + site footer (WEB-3)

Every menu page now carries a compact status strip under the header
(static markup, `role="status"`, populated by deferred `status.js`) and
every page including the menu-less announcement page carries an identical
static footer (GitHub mirror, llms.txt, Coordinator API, Explorer API).

- `67850ac59` — new `website/status.js` (no CDN, no vendor): exactly two
  same-origin requests per cycle (`/explorer-api/api/chain/head` +
  `/explorer-api/api/alerts`), jittered >=30s polling, paused while the
  tab is hidden, capped error backoff that stops after repeated failures
  until reload. Head timestamp parsed as UTC (endpoint returns naive
  timestamps); negative age clamped to zero; >60s negative clock skew
  shows height without age; `STALE_AFTER_MS = 300000` switches the label
  `live`/`stale`. Alerts show muted "alerts unknown" when
  `prometheus_ok` is false, `watcher.present` is false, or
  `watcher.stale` is true; the firing count links to `/alerts.html`.
  Fetch failure renders "network status unavailable" — never throws,
  never blocks rendering.
- `67850ac59` — generator `sync-website-menu.py` extended with
  `aitbc-status` / `aitbc-footer` marker regions; still idempotent and
  `--check`-verified. Status strip is stamped as a sibling before
  `<main>` on the 12 menu pages; footer after the container on all 13.
- `67850ac59` — no build line: no public endpoint reports a commit
  (`/explorer-api/health`'s `version: "2.0.0"` is a static app version,
  deliberately not shown). `docs/ops/website-version-json.md` proposes a
  gitignored `website/version.json` written by an operator-installed
  post-merge hook — proposal only, hook install is its own gated task.
- `67850ac59` — `tests/unit/test_website_menu_consistency.py` extended:
  one status strip with `role="status"` on menu pages, `status.js`
  deferred exactly once, identical footer link set on every page, no CDN
  scripts/styles, `status.js` calls only same-origin paths that exist
  under the repo nginx map's location prefixes.
### CLI: prepay deposit hardening — zero-wei refuse, malformed chain id, loop close (PREPAY-1b)

Three small guards in `aitbc exchange prepay deposit`, none changing the
accepted path:

- `6f5baac4a` — an `--amount-eth` that floors to 0 wei (e.g. 1e-19) is refused in
  both `--print` and `--send` instead of silently building a zero-value
  transaction.
- `6f5baac4a` — a malformed `eth_chainId` result takes the same clean path as a
  missing one (`chain_id_verified: false` under `--print`; the "cannot
  verify the RPC chain id" abort under `--send`) instead of a raw
  ValueError.
- `6f5baac4a` — `_broadcast_raw` now uses `asyncio.run`, so the broadcast event
  loop is closed after the single send instead of being leaked.


